Incidental Pulmonary Nodule
Respiratory System
Asymptomatic presentation, <30mm lesion, Absence of lymphadenopathy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-024
Reason for Visit: Extreme patient anxiety and panic following the receipt of an automated radiology report detailing a "lung nodule," requiring immediate clinical interpretation and reassurance.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a physically well patient experiencing an acute panic attack and severe psychological distress after reading an uninterpreted CT scan report late at night. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically justified to provide expert medical interpretation, rule out acute psychogenic hyperventilation, and establish a rational, evidence-based follow-up plan prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I read my scan results online an hour ago and it says I have a spot on my lung; my chest feels tight and I am terrified I have advanced cancer."
History of Present Illness: The patient underwent a CT scan of the abdomen/lower chest yesterday for an unrelated issue (e.g., suspected renal colic). The report noted an incidental pulmonary nodule. The clinical presentation is entirely asymptomatic regarding the respiratory system. There is a complete absence of systemic complaints such as fever, night sweats, or unintended weight loss. The patient denies any acute respiratory distress, hemoptysis, or chronic cough. The current chest tightness is acute and directly correlates with reading the report (psychogenic).
Functional Status: Fully independent; physically well but psychologically highly distressed.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe acute health anxiety leading to hyperventilation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 mmHg | HR: 110 bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 20 breaths/min | SpO2: 99% on room air | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, but visibly anxious, speaking rapidly, and intermittently hyperventilating. No physical signs of respiratory distress.
Physical Exam: Lung auscultation is completely normal with clear bilateral breath sounds and no focal wheeze or crackles. Palpation of the neck and supraclavicular regions reveals no lymphadenopathy. Review of the radiology report confirms a well-circumscribed lesion measuring 8mm, surrounded entirely by aerated lung parenchyma, with no associated atelectasis or pleural effusion.
Environmental Observations: Patient’s laptop is open to the pathology portal.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Incidental Pulmonary Nodule - Status: Stable (Anxiety Exacerbated)
Differentials:

Acute Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal oxygen saturations, clear lungs, and symptom onset directly linked to reading a report rather than physical pathology.

Active Pulmonary Tuberculosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete lack of systemic symptoms (fever, night sweats, weight loss) and an asymptomatic, isolated small nodule on imaging.

Primary Lung Malignancy (Advanced) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lesion is <30mm, well-circumscribed, without lymphadenopathy, and the patient is entirely asymptomatic.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient has an asymptomatic, incidental pulmonary nodule (<30mm) discovered on imaging, lacking any high-risk features (like lymphadenopathy or spiculation) in the immediate report. The current physiological symptoms (tachycardia, tachypnea, chest tightness) are entirely secondary to acute health anxiety and hyperventilation, not pulmonary pathology. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have left the patient in a state of unmanaged panic, potentially leading to an unnecessary and inappropriate Emergency Department presentation. An urgent after-hours home visit was strictly required to provide authoritative clinical reassurance, perform a normal physical exam to break the anxiety cycle, and explain the standard protocol for incidental nodules.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Provided extensive, detailed clinical reassurance regarding the high prevalence of benign incidental nodules. Guided the patient through slow breathing exercises to resolve the acute hyperventilation.
Positive Behavioral Change: Close the online health portal and do not search the internet for lung nodules tonight; strictly controlling your information intake directly stops the cycle of catastrophic thinking and allows your nervous system to calm down for sleep.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP appointment within 1-2 weeks to review the scan formally and schedule follow-up surveillance imaging (e.g., repeat CT in 3-6 months) as per guidelines.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Seek routine medical care if you develop new, physical symptoms over the coming weeks such as coughing up blood, a new persistent cough, or unexplained weight loss.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: While the vast majority of small incidental nodules are benign (scar tissue, prior mild infections), standard medical practice requires follow-up imaging to confirm stability.
Clinical Course: The nodule requires no immediate treatment; the standard of care is simply to watch it with another scan in a few months to ensure it is not growing.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Return for assessment if your anxiety becomes unmanageable, or if you begin coughing up blood.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Small spots on the lungs are incredibly common and are usually just tiny scars from old colds you didn't even know you had; they do not cause symptoms and usually require no treatment other than a check-up scan.
Actionable Steps: Focus purely on breathing slowly into your belly for the next 10 minutes; this physical action lowers your heart rate and proves to your brain that your lungs are working perfectly.

